<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/R9V07Y1F/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::R9V07Y1F";
        dc:date                "2025-01-20 16:07:13.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::R9V07Y1F";
        pav:authoredOn         "2025-01-20 16:07:13.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/R9V07Y1F/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_nox_expressed_as_nitrogen_due_to_emission_from_fires/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"tendency_of_X\" means derivative of X with respect to time. \"Content\" indicates a quantity per unit area. The \"atmosphere content\" of a quantity refers to the vertical integral from the surface to the top of the atmosphere. For the content between specified levels in the atmosphere, standard names including \"content_of_atmosphere_layer\" are used. The mass is the total mass of the molecules. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Emission\" means emission from a primary source located anywhere within the atmosphere, including at the lower boundary (i.e. earth's surface). \"Emission\" is a process entirely distinct from \"re-emission\" which is used in some standard names. The term \"fires\" means all biomass fires, whether naturally occurring or ignited by humans. \"Nox\" means nitric oxide (NO) and nitrogen dioxide (NO2)."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::R9V07Y1F";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "tendency_of_atmosphere_mass_content_of_nox_expressed_as_nitrogen_due_to_emission_from_fires"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N471/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N471";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N471";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N471/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_hydrogen_chloride_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_hydrogen_chloride_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/HLAT/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G150/>;
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. The chemical formula for hydrogen chloride is HCl."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N471";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_hydrogen_chloride_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/T4QVHCST/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::T4QVHCST";
        dc:date                "2026-06-08 13:55:31.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::T4QVHCST";
        pav:authoredOn         "2026-06-08 13:55:31.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/T4QVHCST/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/change_in_sea_surface_height_due_to_pole_tide/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "\"Sea surface height\" is a time-varying quantity. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. Tides are a significant contributor to the observed sea surface height; the pole tide occurs due to variations in the earth's rotation."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::T4QVHCST";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "change_in_sea_surface_height_due_to_pole_tide"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/ULAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/WHOJ1OG4/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::WHOJ1OG4";
        dc:date                "2016-07-19 15:18:51.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::WHOJ1OG4";
        pav:authoredOn         "2016-07-19 15:18:51.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/WHOJ1OG4/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/sea_surface_secondary_swell_wave_significant_height/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/sea_surface_secondary_swell_wave_significant_height>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/A05/current/EV_WAVES/>;
        skos:definition        "Swell waves are waves on the ocean surface and are the low frequency portion of a bimodal wave frequency spectrum. The secondary swell wave is the second most energetic wave in the low frequency portion of a bimodal wave frequency spectrum. Significant wave height is a statistic computed from wave measurements and corresponds to the average height of the highest one third of the waves, where the height is defined as the vertical distance from a wave trough to the following wave crest."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::WHOJ1OG4";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "sea_surface_secondary_swell_wave_significant_height"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/ULAA/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0070/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CFSN0070";
        dc:date                "2019-06-17 13:47:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CFSN0070";
        pav:authoredOn         "2019-06-17 13:47:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0070/2/>;
        pav:hasVersion         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CFSN0070/1/>;
        pav:version            "2";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/volume_fraction_of_silt_in_soil> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/volume_fraction_of_silt_in_soil/>;
        owl:versionInfo        "2";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P64/current/G763/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/MNGS/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G763/>;
        skos:definition        "\"Volume fraction\" is used in the construction \"volume_fraction_of_X_in_Y\", where X is a material constituent of Y. It is evaluated as the volume of X divided by the volume of Y (including X). It may be expressed as a fraction, a percentage, or any other dimensionless representation of a fraction."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CFSN0070";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "volume_fraction_of_silt_in_soil"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/UUUU/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N452/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::CF12N452";
        dc:date                "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::CF12N452";
        pav:authoredOn         "2009-07-06 15:41:29.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/CF12N452/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/mole_concentration_of_ethane_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/mole_concentration_of_ethane_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:broader           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P04/current/G140/> , <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P02/current/AALK/>;
        skos:definition        "Mole concentration means number of moles per unit volume, also called \"molarity\", and is used in the construction mole_concentration_of_X_in_Y, where X is a material constituent of Y.  A chemical species denoted by X may be described by a single term such as 'nitrogen' or a phrase such as 'nox_expressed_as_nitrogen'. The chemical formula for ethane is C2H6. Ethane is a member of the group of hydrocarbons known as alkanes.  There are standard names for the alkane group as well as for some of the individual species."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::CF12N452";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "mole_concentration_of_ethane_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/MLM3/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/GOLXU441/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::GOLXU441";
        dc:date                "2016-12-13 16:52:07.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::GOLXU441";
        pav:authoredOn         "2016-12-13 16:52:07.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/GOLXU441/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_carbon_dioxide_expressed_as_carbon_due_to_anthropogenic_land_use_or_land_cover_change/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/surface_upward_mass_flux_of_carbon_dioxide_expressed_as_carbon_due_to_anthropogenic_land_use_or_land_cover_change>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The surface called \"surface\" means the lower boundary of the atmosphere. \"Upward\" indicates a vector component which is positive when directed upward (negative downward). In accordance with common usage in geophysical disciplines, \"flux\" implies per unit area, called \"flux density\" in physics. The phrase \"expressed_as\" is used in the construction A_expressed_as_B, where B is a chemical constituent of A. It means that the quantity indicated by the standard name is calculated solely with respect to the B contained in A, neglecting all other chemical constituents of A. The chemical formula for carbon dioxide is CO2. The specification of a physical process by the phrase \"due_to_\" process means that the quantity named is a single term in a sum of terms which together compose the general quantity named by omitting the phrase. \"Anthropogenic\" means influenced, caused, or created by human activity.  \"Anthropogenic land use change\" means human changes to land, excluding forest regrowth. It includes fires ignited by humans for the purpose of land use change and the processes of eventual disposal and decomposition of wood products such as paper, cardboard, furniture and timber for construction."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::GOLXU441";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "surface_upward_mass_flux_of_carbon_dioxide_expressed_as_carbon_due_to_anthropogenic_land_use_or_land_cover_change"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/KSP2/> .

<http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/B8VGLMWX/>
        rdf:type               skos:Concept;
        dce:identifier         "SDN:P07::B8VGLMWX";
        dc:date                "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        dc:identifier          "SDN:P07::B8VGLMWX";
        pav:authoredOn         "2018-02-12 16:53:18.0";
        pav:hasCurrentVersion  <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P07/current/B8VGLMWX/1/>;
        pav:version            "1";
        void:inDataset         <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/.well-known/void>;
        owl:deprecated         "false";
        owl:sameAs             <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/standard_name/integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_135Cs_in_air/> , <http://mmisw.org/ont/cf/parameter/integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_135Cs_in_air>;
        owl:versionInfo        "1";
        skos:altLabel          "";
        skos:definition        "The phrase \"integral_wrt_X_of_Y\" means int Y dX. The data variable should have an axis for X specifying the limits of the integral as bounds. The phrase \"wrt\" means \"with respect to\". \"Radioactivity\" means the number of radioactive decays of a material per second. \"Radioactivity concentration\" means radioactivity per unit volume of the medium. \"Cs\" means the element \"cesium\" and \"135Cs\" is the isotope \"cesium-135\" with a half-life of 8.39e+08 days."@en;
        skos:notation          "SDN:P07::B8VGLMWX";
        skos:note              "accepted"@en;
        skos:prefLabel         "integral_wrt_time_of_radioactivity_concentration_of_135Cs_in_air"@en;
        skos:related           <http://vocab.nerc.ac.uk/collection/P06/current/BSM3/> .

